task: intent-per-class-not-aggregate
agent: opus5-intent
started: 2026-09-05
branch: feature/intent-per-class-not-aggregate
worktree: ../okay-wt-perclass

why: the consumer's finding, and it lands on me. They filled a corpus
  hole, one class grew to 137 of 184 rows, a probe leaned to the
  majority, and "сегодня в москве шёл дождь" came back as a REQUEST at
  0.90 — while HEADLINE ACCURACY ROSE, 95.8% to 96.2%. Accuracy on an
  imbalanced corpus rewards predicting the biggest class, so the
  aggregate can go UP while a class dies. A regression test caught it;
  the average never would have.

  Every aggregate this module publishes has that exposure, starting
  with the shipped model's 76.7% in a doc comment, a module page and
  two changelog entries.

what: print per-class numbers and the class BALANCE beside every total
  the shipped model quotes; assert the per-class floor rather than
  only the mean, so a class dying fails a test. `Eval` already
  computes per-class scores — this is a reporting lane, not a
  measurement one.

files: okay-intent tests (TestModels, TestSecondAuthor),
  Models.scala doc, docs/modules/okay-intent.md,
  specs/intent-classify.md, BACKLOG.md
